From: Manifold build
Subject: [PATCH] Allow a recvonly track to send RTCP when a media handler is chained

impl::Track::outgoing refuses any outgoing message on a RecvOnly or Inactive track
unless it is typed Message::Control, and only types RTCP as Control when NO media
handler is chained. Chaining RtcpReceivingSession -- required for rtcRequestKeyframe
to reach the wire -- therefore makes receive-side NACK unsendable.

Applied by scripts/build_libdatachannel.sh against libdatachannel v0.24.5.
Upstreamable: the `!handler` condition contradicts the comment on the line it guards.
---
--- a/src/impl/track.cpp
+++ b/src/impl/track.cpp
@@ -170,14 +170,26 @@
 bool Track::outgoing(message_ptr message) {
 	if (mIsClosed)
 		throw std::runtime_error("Track is closed");
 
 	auto handler = getMediaHandler();
 
-	// If there is no handler, the track expects RTP or RTCP packets
-	if (!handler && IsRtcp(*message))
+	// Outgoing RTCP is control traffic whatever the media direction, and whether or not a media
+	// handler happens to be chained.
+	//
+	// MANIFOLD PATCH. Upstream additionally required `!handler` here. That silently disabled
+	// this exemption for any recvonly track carrying an RtcpReceivingSession -- which is exactly
+	// the configuration a receiver needs in order to send PLI, since Track::requestKeyframe
+	// requires a handler. The effect was that enabling keyframe requests disabled receive-side
+	// NACK, with no way to send one through any public C or C++ API: Track::send lands here and
+	// is refused, and the int->shared_ptr<Track> lookup a custom MediaHandler would need lives
+	// in an anonymous namespace in capi.cpp.
+	//
+	// The comment upstream put on the line below states the intent this patch restores.
+	// See docs/WHEP_LOADED_NETWORK_FINDINGS.md 10.3 in the Manifold repo.
+	if (IsRtcp(*message))
 		message->type = Message::Control; // to allow sending RTCP packets irrelevant of direction
 
 	auto dir = direction();
 	if ((dir == Description::Direction::RecvOnly || dir == Description::Direction::Inactive) &&
 	    message->type != Message::Control) {
 		COUNTER_MEDIA_BAD_DIRECTION++;
